.TH MT-LOGO-RENDER 1 "2026-09-19" "HEE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mt-logo-render \- real Pillow renderer for the mt-logo recipe schema
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B render.py
.I RECIPE.json
.B \-o
.I OUT.png
[\fB\-\-no\-qr\fR]
[\fB\-\-qr\-payload\fR \fITEXT\fR]
[\fB\-\-qr\-primary\fR]
.br
.B render.py
.B \-\-read\-anchor
.I OUT.png
.SH DESCRIPTION
Renders a parametric logo from a JSON recipe, real 32\-bit RGBA
output. Every render self\-verifies: a real scannable QR corner badge
and matching PNG metadata, both encoding the recipe's canonical
SHA256 id \-\- see
.BR hee-qr (1)
to read either back.
.PP
This is the real, missing pixel\-rendering half of the vendored
.B upstream/mt\-logo\-render
(recipe.rs), which only ever defined the data model, never the actual
rendering code.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
Pillow and
.B qrcode
are imported at module level, so both are required before even
\fB\-\-help\fR will run \-\- without them the tool traces back on
import rather than printing usage. On Debian:
.B python3\-pil
and
.BR python3\-qrcode ,
no pip needed.
.B zbar\-tools
is not needed to render, only to verify a render.
.SH QR SIZING
The QR is drawn at an exact integer number of pixels per module and is
.B never
resampled; it is padded out to its target size inside the white
quiet\-zone box. Before 2026\-09\-19 it was drawn oversized and then
shrunk to the target with NEAREST, which at a fractional factor drops
module rows unevenly \-\- the PNG still looked like a QR and silently
did not decode, and not even monotonically in canvas size (issue 774).
.PP
Two pixels per module is the floor, measured against zbarimg: at one
pixel per module a decode is luck. A small canvas therefore gets a QR
taking a larger share of it than the nominal 22%, and if not even one
pixel per module fits, the QR is omitted and the reason is printed on
stderr rather than a code being drawn that cannot be scanned.
.SH RECIPE SCHEMA
.TP
.B shape
circle | square | triangle | hex
.TP
.B size
"WxH", 16..4096 each dimension
.TP
.B base_color
"#RGB" / "#RRGGBB" or a PIL\-recognized name
.TP
.B accent_color
same format, optional
.TP
.B fill
solid | pie:DEG | split:N | stripe:N
.TP
.B mark
check | x | dot
.TP
.B badge
corner_dot | corner_check
.TP
.B label
<=4 chars, centered
.TP
.B glyph
any unicode, centered (real color emoji via NotoColorEmoji when
applicable, not a missing\-glyph box)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " OUT.png"
Output path. Required unless \fB\-\-read\-anchor\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-qr
Skip the scannable QR corner badge.
.TP
.BI \-\-qr\-payload " TEXT"
Put
.I TEXT
in the QR instead of the recipe's anchor URL. Default, and the
behaviour when the flag is absent:
\fBhttps://spencer.blog.tcos.us/?hash=\fR\fIRECIPE_ID\fR. Used for
payloads that are not the anchor, such as the keyhole token
\fBHK1:\fR\fIkid\fR\fB:\fR\fIhalf_a\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-qr\-primary
Inverse layout: the QR becomes the main element, centered and filling
most of the canvas, and the rendered logo becomes the corner badge.
For a token scanned off a phone, where the default 22%\-of\-canvas
corner QR is not a usable target. The badge is placed beside the QR,
never over its modules. Absent this flag the layout is exactly the
default one.
.TP
.BI \-\-read\-anchor " OUT.png"
Read the embedded recipe/hash back out instead of rendering. Same as
.BR hee-qr (1)'s
PNG\-metadata path.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
render.py hee\-recipe.json \-o hee\-logo.png
render.py \-\-read\-anchor hee\-logo.png
render.py hee\-recipe.json \-o token.png \-\-qr\-primary \\
    \-\-qr\-payload "HK1:KID:HALFA"
.fi
.RE
.SH VERIFICATION
.RS
.nf
sha256sum OUT.png       real, reproducible, bit\-for\-bit across runs
zbarimg \-\-raw OUT.png   scans the real QR out of the pixels

tests/test_rrr_qr.py renders at 128..1024 and asserts zbarimg reads
back exactly the payload that went in. It needs python3\-qrcode and
zbar\-tools, skips when either is absent, and runs in CI where both
are installed.
.fi
.RE
